wined3d: Present a COPY swapchain's last frame again shortly after.

Under Xwayland, a frame presented through a Vulkan swapchain to a Wine
client window can stay unshown until the next present on that window
(Mesa's X11 WSI; the GL renderer and software presents do not show it, and
Mesa's present mode, image count and Xwayland options do not change it).
Applications that present only when something changes then show the frame
before the latest. In Affinity, under WPF, a click highlighted a tool or a
layer only once the next thing was clicked, and a studio switch left its
panels empty until a second click.

After a windowed, immediate-interval COPY present, if the back buffer has not
been written since, present it again 5 ms later and once more 45 ms after
that. With a COPY swap effect the back buffer still holds exactly that frame,
so the extra presents show nothing new; they push the frame out. Writes are
counted per texture in wined3d_texture_invalidate_location(), which every
write path goes through.

The re-present runs on the command stream thread when its queue is empty.
The application thread changes and frees swapchain state outside the command
stream, under the wined3d mutex, so the re-present takes the mutex with a
try-lock and checks the queue is still empty: whatever such a change leaves
pending is in the queue. It skips windows that are not visible, and each
swapchain keeps its own.

In a WPF test that changes a window's color every 2 s, the screen showed
each color as it was set; without this, the one before.

